      "id": 1,
      "text": "Ione was named Ioneville in 1853 when Thomas Rickey subdivided land along the stage road from Sacramento.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitamador.com/p/towns--regions/ione",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The railroad reached Ione in 1910, linking it to Metaline Falls and Newport via the Idaho and Washington Northern Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ione%2C_Washington",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Blackwell's Panhandle Lumber Company opened the Inland Northwest's first all‑electric sawmill in Ione, sparking a timber boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ione%2C_Washington",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Ione sits on the west bank of the Pend Oreille River as it flows north toward Canada, surrounded by steep Selkirk Mountains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ione%2C_Washington",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Cement Mountain rises to 3,003 feet just north of town, while Molybdenite Mountain across the river soars to 6,788 feet.",
